Let \(q=x^{-1}\in(0,1)\). Expanding each denominator geometrically gives
\[
\frac{1}{x^{3^n}-x^{-3^n}}
=\frac{q^{3^n}}{1-q^{2\cdot3^n}}
=\sum_{j=0}^{\infty}q^{(2j+1)3^n}.
\]
For any positive odd integer \(m\), the term \(q^m\) occurs once for each \(n\) with \(3^n\mid m\), hence \(\nu_3(m)+1\) times, where \(\nu_3(m)\) is the exponent of \(3\) in \(m\). Since all terms are nonnegative, they may be regrouped. Thus
\[
\boxed{\displaystyle
\sum_{n=0}^{\infty}\frac{1}{x^{3^n}-x^{-3^n}}
=\sum_{\substack{m\ge1\\m\ \mathrm{odd}}}
\frac{\nu_3(m)+1}{x^m}}
\qquad (x>1).
\]
The series converges, since \(\nu_3(m)+1\le m+1\) and \(\sum_{m\ge1}(m+1)x^{-m}\) converges.

Final answer: \(\displaystyle \sum_{m\ge1,\ m\text{ odd}}(\nu_3(m)+1)x^{-m}\).

